    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n36002" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Phĭlădelphus</span></b> ,
		i, m., = <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Φιλάδελφος</span> (loving one's brother or sister), <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>a Grecian and Roman surname.</i>
		</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b> Ptolemaeus Philadelphus,
				<i>a king of Egypt</i>, <i><span class="en">Plin. 36, 7,
				14, § 67</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Gell. 6,
				17, 3</span></i>.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b> Annius Philadelphus, <i><span class="en">Cic.
				Phil. 13, 12, 26</span></i>.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>III.</b> Philadelphus, <i>a slave of
			Atticus</i>, <i><span class="en">Cic. Att. 1, 11, 2</span></i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">L. CALPVRNIVS L. L. PHILADELPHVS,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Inscr. Maff. Mus. Veron. 274, 9</span></i>
			.</div>
